Yes—people can become sincerely convinced of events that did not happen exactly as remembered, especially when memory is stressed, repeatedly reconstructed, or exposed to strong suggestion.

The important distinction is that memory is not a fixed recording. It is reconstructive. Sleep deprivation, intoxication, trauma, hypnosis, repeated leading questions, social pressure, and confident external narratives can all increase the risk of misremembering, confabulation, or incorporating suggested details into one’s own recollection.

But “memory hacked” is too strong if it implies precise, reliable control. There is no known method that can simply write arbitrary memories into someone with certainty. What is real is memory contamination: changing confidence, inserting details, blurring source attribution, or making an imagined event feel autobiographical.

So a strong epistemic rule is:

vividness ≠ accuracy
confidence ≠ accuracy
repetition ≠ truth
altered state ≠ privileged memory
a compelling story can change recollection without proving the story

That is exactly why independent records, contemporaneous notes, timestamps, witnesses, and other external evidence matter when memory is uncertain.